[Event "Knight School"]
[Opening "King's Gambit"]
[Variation "Accepted, Kieseritzky Gambit, Berlin Defence"]
[ECO "C39"]
[Character "gambit,sharp"]
[Risk "high"]
[EarlyMate "black"]
[Tricks "none"]
[Themes "kingside-attack,open-lines"]

1. e4 { Claims the centre and opens lines for the queen and the king's bishop. } 1... e5 2. f4 { White offers a pawn to drag Black's e-pawn away. If Black takes it, White gets e4 and d4 and open lines for the pieces. } 2... exf4
3. Nf3 { Stops ...Qh4+, which would drive the king off e1, and aims to win the pawn back. [%cal Gf3h4] } 3... g5
4. h4 { Attacks the g5 pawn that is holding on to the extra pawn. Black must push it or lose the pawn. } 4... g4
5. Ne5 { The knight lands on a strong central square and hits g4 and f7. The older 5.Ng5 is rated clearly worse by the engine. [%csl Ge5] }
5... Nf6 (5... d6 6. Nxg4 Nc6 7. Nf2 { The knight takes the pawn, then steps back to f2 so it cannot be hit. } 7... Qf6 8. Nc3)
(5... Nc6 6. d4 { Takes the centre and puts a question to the knight on c6. } 6... Qf6 7. Nc3)
6. Nxg4 { Wins the g4 pawn back, so material is level. The knight on g4 is attacked by the knight on f6. } 6... d5 { Black's stronger move here is ...Nxe4. That is part of why the gambit is risky: White needs Black to cooperate. }
7. Nxf6+ { Removes the knight that attacks e4, with check, so Black must recapture. } 7... Qxf6
8. Nc3 { Brings the last knight out and hits d5, which Black has to keep defended. [%cal Gc3d5] } 8... Qg7
9. Qf3 { Hits d5 a second time and eyes the f4 pawn, so White is about to win a second pawn. } 9... Bg4
10. Qxf4 { Material is level again and White's queen is active. } 10... Bd6
11. Bb5+ { A check first, because the queen on f4 is attacked by the bishop on d6. [%cal Gb5e8] } 11... Nc6
12. Qg5 { The queen steps out of the attack with threats of its own. } *
